Serial Overview
Serial

!!!Introduction

The <*Serial*> library (<*PocoSerial*>, namespace <[Poco::Serial]>) provides access to serial
ports through a single class, <[Poco::Serial::SerialPort]>. A port is opened by device name and
configured with a line speed, a character size, a parity setting, a number of stop bits and
optional hardware (RTS/CTS) flow control. Incoming data is read through an internal buffer,
while a write is passed straight to the device; the arrival of data can be awaited with a
timeout, RTS can be driven manually, and RS-485 mode can be configured where the platform
supports it.

Unless a logger is passed to the constructor, the port writes its debug messages to the logger
<[IO.SerialPort]>.

Dependencies: Foundation.


!!!Building Serial

The CMake option <*ENABLE_SERIAL*> is on by default and off with <*POCO_MINIMAL_BUILD*>.

    cmake -B build -DENABLE_SERIAL=ON
    cmake --build build

The library is not supported for the emscripten target. <*ENABLE_SERIAL*> is turned off
automatically when building for emscripten, and turning it on for that target is not supported.


!!!Reading from and Writing to a Port

The constructor that takes a device name opens the port; the default constructor leaves it
closed for a later call to <[open()]>, and a port that is not open throws
<[Poco::IllegalStateException]>. <[isOpen()]> reports whether a port is open. The destructor
closes the port.

A <[SerialPort]> cannot be copied, but it can be moved. The move constructor takes over the
device, the buffered input and the logger of the other port; move assignment takes over the
device and the buffered input, and keeps the logger of the port that is assigned to, because
that logger is a reference. The port that is moved from is left closed and without a buffer,
so that only destroying it or assigning to it is supported; <[open()]> on it throws
<[Poco::IllegalStateException]>.

<[poll()]> waits for incoming data for up to the given <[Poco::Timespan]> and returns whether
data is available. A negative timeout is treated as zero, so the call returns at once, and on
Windows a positive wait shorter than a millisecond is rounded up to a millisecond, because a
total read timeout of zero means to the driver that there is no timeout at all. Once
<[poll()]> has returned <[true]> the data is in the internal buffer on Windows, while on POSIX
platforms it is fetched by the following read, so <[available()]> may still be 0 there.

<[read()]> without a timeout blocks until data arrives, for as long as the port stays
connected. Because <[open()]> asks the device for at least one character per read, a read of
zero bytes on an open port means that the peer has hung up, which is what unplugging a USB
serial adapter does; all four <[read()]> overloads report it as <[Poco::IOException]>.
<[write()]> is not buffered: it passes the data to the device and returns once all of it has
been written.

Data is read into an internal buffer of 4096 bytes, or of the size passed to the constructor;
<[available()]> returns how much of it has not been read yet. A buffer size of zero is
rejected with <[Poco::InvalidArgumentException]>.

    #include "Poco/Serial/SerialPort.h"
    #include "Poco/Timespan.h"
    #include "Poco/Exception.h"
    #include <iostream>
    #include <string>

    int main()
    {
        try
        {
            Poco::Serial::SerialPort port("/dev/ttyS0", 9600, "8N1");
            port.write("AT\r");
            if (port.poll(Poco::Timespan(2, 0)))
            {
                std::string response;
                port.read(response);
                std::cout << response << std::endl;
            }
            port.close();
        }
        catch (const Poco::Exception& exc)
        {
            std::cerr << exc.displayText() << std::endl;
            return 1;
        }
        return 0;
    }

The program links with PocoSerial and PocoFoundation.


!!!Exclusive Use and Concurrency

On Windows a port is opened for exclusive use, so a second attempt to open the same device
fails. POSIX platforms do not lock the device, so two ports can be open on it at the same
time, and the bytes that arrive are then split between their readers.

<[SerialPort]> is not thread-safe. <[read()]>, <[poll()]> and <[available()]> all work on the
receive buffer, so one port must not be read from more than one thread at a time: two such
calls can interleave and advance the buffer past its end. <[write()]>, <[drain()]> and
<[setRTS()]> do not touch the buffer and may be called while another thread reads.

On Windows the operations on one port are serialised, because the handle is not overlapped: a
thread waiting in <[read()]> also delays a <[write()]>, <[drain()]> or <[setRTS()]> issued from
another thread. On POSIX platforms a blocked read does not delay a write.


!!!Device Names

A device name is the name of the port's device file. On POSIX platforms it is something
like <[/dev/ttyS0]>, <[/dev/ttyUSB0]> or <[/dev/tty.usbserial-XXX]>, on Windows <[COM1]>. A
device name that does not already begin with a backslash is prefixed with \\.\ by the Windows
implementation, so that port numbers above 9 work as well.


!!!Line Parameters

The parameters argument of <[open()]> is a string of three characters: the character size, the
parity and the number of stop bits, for example <[8N1]> for eight bit characters, no parity and
one stop bit. A string of any other length is rejected with <[Poco::InvalidArgumentException]>,
and so is a character the platform does not accept. Parity may be given in upper or in lower
case. The accepted characters differ between the platforms:

  - POSIX: character size <[5]>, <[6]>, <[7]> or <[8]>; parity <[N]> (none), <[E]> (even) or
    <[O]> (odd); <[1]> or <[2]> stop bits.
  - Windows: the same character sizes; parity <[N]>, <[E]>, <[O]>, <[M]> (mark) or <[S]>
    (space); <[1]> or <[2]> stop bits, and <[5]> in the third position for 1.5 stop bits.
    Windows rejects 5 data bits with 2 stop bits, and 1.5 stop bits with 6, 7 or 8 data bits.

A device can accept the request and apply only part of it. On POSIX platforms the character
size, the parity and the number of stop bits are therefore read back once they have been set,
and a difference from what was asked for makes <[open()]> throw <[Poco::IOException]>. The
line speed is not read back, because a device may round it.

The line speed is given in bits per second and differs between the platforms as well. POSIX
accepts 50, 75, 110, 134, 150, 200, 300, 600, 1200, 1800, 2400, 4800, 9600, 19200, 38400,
57600, 115200 and 230400, and 460800, 500000, 576000, 921600, 1000000, 1152000, 1500000,
2000000, 2500000, 3000000, 3500000 and 4000000 where the platform defines them. Windows
accepts 110, 300, 600, 1200, 2400, 4800, 9600, 14400, 19200, 38400, 57600 and 115200. A speed
of -1, the default, leaves the device at the speed it is configured for.


!!!Draining the Output

<[drain()]> waits for the output written to the port to be transmitted, and it takes no
timeout: an application whose peer may stop reading needs a timeout of its own. On POSIX
platforms it waits until the device has transmitted the pending output, and therefore blocks
for as long as the receiver does not read it. On Windows it waits for the queued writes to
complete, which does not guarantee that the transmit buffer of the device is empty, so
switching the direction of an RS-485 transceiver immediately after <[drain()]> can truncate the
end of the frame there; a driver that implements no flush at all returns without doing
anything.


!!!Manual RTS Control

<[setRTS()]> raises or lowers the RTS line of an open port, except on Windows under
<[FLOW_RTSCTS]>, where the driver owns the line and the call throws <[Poco::IOException]>.
<[getRTS()]> reports the state of the line, but not in the same way on both platforms: on
POSIX it reads the line from the device, while on Windows it returns the value that
<[open()]> or the last call to <[setRTS()]> established.


!!!RS-485 Mode

<[configureRS485()]> enables and configures RS-485 mode. On platforms that have no interface
for it at all, among them macOS and Windows, the call throws
<[Poco::NotImplementedException]>; where the interface exists but the device refuses the
request, which is what Linux does on hardware without RS-485 support, it throws
<[Poco::IOException]>. <[SerialPort::RS485Params]> carries the flags, the RTS delays before and
after sending, and the GPIO pin.

The GPIO pin is passed to the kernel in the <[gpio_pin]> member of <[struct serial_rs485]>,
which only a patched <[linux/serial.h]> provides (<[SER_RS485_USE_GPIO]>). With an unpatched
header, building the library with the macro <*POCO_SERIAL_ENABLE_BEAGLEBONE_RS485_HACK*> passes
the pin in the first padding field of that structure instead, which is where the BeagleBone
kernel expects it.


!!!Testing

The test suite needs no serial hardware. Some of its cases touch no device at all; the others
open a pseudo terminal, which has a termios line discipline and nothing behind it, and skip
themselves with a warning where no pseudo terminal can be created. The cases that use a pseudo
terminal are POSIX only.
